### Retries and poison queue – failure scenario

`host.json`: `maxDequeueCount = 5`, `visibilityTimeout = 10 s`. If the worker raises (API unreachable, timeout, HTTP 5xx, unreadable message), the message becomes visible again and is retried; after **5 failed attempts** the Functions runtime moves it to **`inference-requests-poison`** (dead-letter queue), where it can be inspected and replayed.

Demonstrated scenario: a message that is not valid JSON was put in the queue
(`az storage message put -q inference-requests --content "bm90IGpzb24="`, i.e. "not json" in base64) → 5 failed attempts → message in the poison queue ([`docs/pipeline_poison.png`](docs/pipeline_poison.png)).

| Scenario | Evidence |
|---|---|
| Valid upload → result in < 1 min | [`docs/pipeline_output.png`](docs/pipeline_output.png) |
| Invalid upload → `rejected/` + reason | [`docs/pipeline_rejected.png`](docs/pipeline_rejected.png) |
| Poisoned message → `-poison` queue | [`docs/pipeline_poison.png`](docs/pipeline_poison.png) |
| New upload appears on the dashboard automatically | [`docs/dashboard.png`](docs/dashboard.png) |

### No secret in the repository

All Azure access from the Function App uses its **system-assigned managed identity** (`DefaultAzureCredential`): `Storage Blob Data Owner` and `Storage Queue Data Contributor` on the storage account, **Cosmos DB Built-in Data Contributor** (data-plane role) on the Cosmos account. Queue trigger and output use an identity-based connection (`PIPELINE_STORAGE__queueServiceUri`). Settings are application settings, never in Git.

